1
00:00:01,376 --> 00:00:04,505
Item 28: your pet name for me.

2
00:00:05,672 --> 00:00:07,966
Time's running out on this.
You need to make a decision.

3
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:10,344
I submitted you a notarized list.

4
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:13,972
I'm sorry, but "Gollum"
and "Flakey" are not acceptable.

5
00:00:14,556 --> 00:00:16,808
Well, you don't like
"Princess Corncob"

6
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:20,145
you don't like "Fester,"
you're just impossible to please.

7
00:00:20,312 --> 00:00:21,730
We'll come back to that one.

8
00:00:22,147 --> 00:00:23,357
This brings us to the final item

9
00:00:23,524 --> 00:00:25,817
in our annual
State of the Relationship summit.

10
00:00:25,984 --> 00:00:28,654
Item 29: Valentine's Day.

11
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:31,823
Ohh! The worst for last.

12
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:34,701
Classic Flakey.

13
00:00:35,661 --> 00:00:37,496
Before you get upset,

14
00:00:37,663 --> 00:00:39,414
I believe I've come up with
a way for us to celebrate

15
00:00:39,581 --> 00:00:41,166
the occasion that we both can enjoy.

16
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:43,752
People usually start
a meeting with a joke,

17
00:00:43,919 --> 00:00:46,171
but you go ahead, end with one.

18
00:00:47,297 --> 00:00:49,383
I propose we spend a weekend

19
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:51,343
at a bed-and-breakfast
in Napa Valley.

20
00:00:51,510 --> 00:00:54,053
I hate every word in that sentence.

21
00:00:55,764 --> 00:00:58,559
Including "in" "at," "we" and "a."

22
00:00:59,643 --> 00:01:01,144
Come on, Sheldon. Why not?

23
00:01:01,311 --> 00:01:03,855
For starters,
a bed-and-breakfast forces you

24
00:01:04,022 --> 00:01:06,984
-to eat with strangers at your table.
-One step ahead of you.

25
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:08,860
Bernadette and Wolowitz
are going with us.

26
00:01:09,027 --> 00:01:11,405
Very well. What are
the sleeping arrangements?

27
00:01:11,572 --> 00:01:14,116
We've only been dating
for three years.

28
00:01:14,283 --> 00:01:17,077
If we were to share a room,
people might talk.

29
00:01:18,453 --> 00:01:19,621
I got you your own room.

30
00:01:19,788 --> 00:01:21,623
What if my room has
a claw-foot bathtub?

31
00:01:21,790 --> 00:01:24,626
It doesn't. I know it makes you feel
like you're bathing inside a monster.

32
00:01:26,336 --> 00:01:29,840
Look, I appreciate the effort,
but I'm still unclear

33
00:01:30,007 --> 00:01:33,176
how this trip is supposed
to be enjoyable for me.

34
00:01:33,343 --> 00:01:35,220
We're going to have
Valentine's Day dinner

35
00:01:35,387 --> 00:01:39,016
on a fully functioning vintage train.

36
00:01:39,182 --> 00:01:41,852
Vintage? Be specific.

37
00:01:42,019 --> 00:01:44,187
An Alcoa FA-4
diesel locomotive

38
00:01:44,354 --> 00:01:46,607
leading a train
of meticulously restored

39
00:01:46,773 --> 00:01:49,276
1915 Pullman first-class coaches.

40
00:01:49,735 --> 00:01:51,361
Wow.

41
00:01:52,821 --> 00:01:54,656
I'm feeling the urge to hug you.

42
00:01:55,866 --> 00:01:58,368
And one... and two...

43
00:01:58,535 --> 00:02:01,371
All right, Gollum, we're good.

44
00:02:01,663 --> 00:02:03,498
(THEME SONG)

45
00:02:25,771 --> 00:02:27,481
Are you sure you guys don't
want to come with us to Napa?

46
00:02:27,648 --> 00:02:28,857
You could probably still get a room.

47
00:02:29,024 --> 00:02:31,443
No, I think we're just gonna
have a quiet weekend at home.

48
00:02:31,610 --> 00:02:32,653
The Locomotive Manipulation

49
00:02:32,819 --> 00:02:35,447
Plus, I'm not sure it's a great idea
to take Penny to where wine comes from.

50
00:02:38,408 --> 00:02:39,576
What? It's a joke.

51
00:02:39,743 --> 00:02:41,995
Oh, come on.
We bust on each other.

52
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:44,289
I wear dorky glasses,
you might have a problem.

53
00:02:44,456 --> 00:02:46,166
It's all for laughs.

54
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:50,462
That would really piss me off if
I didn't have a buzz going on.

55
00:02:54,299 --> 00:02:58,637
Howard, which pocket watch will you
be wearing for dinner on the train?

56
00:03:04,476 --> 00:03:05,727
I'm sorry, what?

57
00:03:06,645 --> 00:03:08,897
I'm afraid if we wear
the same pocket watch,

58
00:03:09,064 --> 00:03:10,649
it will be embarrassing.

59
00:03:12,317 --> 00:03:14,611
I don't own a pocket watch.

60
00:03:15,445 --> 00:03:17,114
Oh, my.

61
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:20,909
Well, then my apologies
for bringing up this sore spot.

62
00:03:24,496 --> 00:03:26,373
Since you two are gonna be
around for Valentine's,

63
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:27,666
would you mind
watching Cinnamon?

64
00:03:27,833 --> 00:03:29,918
You have Valentine's plans?

65
00:03:30,085 --> 00:03:32,879
Which came out sounding way
more surprised than I meant.

66
00:03:34,131 --> 00:03:35,757
Let me try that again.
You have Val--

67
00:03:35,924 --> 00:03:39,720
-See? I can't do it.
-I don't have plans,

68
00:03:39,886 --> 00:03:42,723
which is why I booked time
on the big telescope that night.

69
00:03:42,889 --> 00:03:45,851
Well, an evening looking at the
stars, that's still kind of romantic.

70
00:03:46,017 --> 00:03:47,269
Except I'll be alone.

71
00:03:47,436 --> 00:03:50,105
I'm trying to put lipstick
on a pig here. Work with me.

72
00:03:52,149 --> 00:03:53,608
-We'd be happy to watch Cinnamon.
-Yeah.

73
00:03:53,775 --> 00:03:55,610
Thank you. Oh, and I'd like
for at least one of us

74
00:03:55,777 --> 00:03:57,696
to see some action, so if you
guys happen to have sex,

75
00:03:57,863 --> 00:03:59,614
it's cool if she stays in the room.

76
00:04:00,824 --> 00:04:03,201
Same goes for the
two of you with Amy.

77
00:04:16,423 --> 00:04:18,091
What do you think, Sheldon?

78
00:04:18,257 --> 00:04:20,135
It's magnificent.

79
00:04:20,302 --> 00:04:22,637
This is going to be
the best Valentine's Day ever.

80
00:04:22,804 --> 00:04:24,514
I'm so glad you like it.

81
00:04:24,681 --> 00:04:26,349
I'm prepared to say I love it,

82
00:04:26,516 --> 00:04:29,186
as soon as I confirm there
are no hobos aboard.

83
00:04:32,856 --> 00:04:34,566
OK.

84
00:04:35,567 --> 00:04:37,527
Raj, I got it.

85
00:04:37,944 --> 00:04:39,237
Bye.

86
00:04:40,864 --> 00:04:42,783
That was your daddy.

87
00:04:43,784 --> 00:04:47,370
He wanted me to say that he misses--
Why am I doing this?

88
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:50,707
Happy Valentine's Day!

89
00:04:50,874 --> 00:04:53,043
Flowers and chocolates?

90
00:04:53,210 --> 00:04:55,754
Somebody's trying
to get me out of my panties.

91
00:04:56,963 --> 00:04:59,299
Don't be surprised if you
find five chocolates missing

92
00:04:59,466 --> 00:05:01,510
and three gross coconut
ones with a bite taken out.

93
00:05:03,303 --> 00:05:05,222
It came that way when I bought it.

94
00:05:05,806 --> 00:05:07,516
Got you a little something, too.

95
00:05:07,682 --> 00:05:10,018
Oh, jewelry!

96
00:05:10,936 --> 00:05:13,688
Oh, my God. Lakers tickets?!

97
00:05:16,441 --> 00:05:18,026
It gets better. Instead of me,
you can take someone

98
00:05:18,193 --> 00:05:20,612
-who will actually enjoy it.
-Ohh!

99
00:05:20,904 --> 00:05:23,281
You are the best boyfriend ever.

100
00:05:23,448 --> 00:05:26,201
Thank you. Seriously,
please don't make me go.

101
00:05:29,996 --> 00:05:31,498
Should we take this little
party to the bedroom?

102
00:05:31,665 --> 00:05:33,750
Don't have to. We have
the whole place to ourselves.

103
00:05:33,917 --> 00:05:36,378
-That's true.
-In fact, if you want,

104
00:05:36,545 --> 00:05:39,005
we can do it right here
on Sheldon's spot.

105
00:05:41,174 --> 00:05:44,761
That is the least sexy thing
anyone's ever said to me.

106
00:05:45,846 --> 00:05:47,848
-To the bedroom!
-Yeah!

107
00:05:52,561 --> 00:05:55,605
And for the entrée, tonight's
special is a seafood risotto.

108
00:05:56,022 --> 00:05:58,316
-Do you have any questions?
-I do.

109
00:05:58,483 --> 00:06:02,361
Does this train car have
the original link-and-pin coupler

110
00:06:02,529 --> 00:06:04,531
or the Miller Hook and platform?

111
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:07,617
I'm sorry, I meant
questions about the food.

112
00:06:07,784 --> 00:06:09,494
Oh, of course.

113
00:06:09,661 --> 00:06:12,622
Is the seafood risotto being
served on a train car

114
00:06:12,789 --> 00:06:14,165
with the original
link-and-pin coupler

115
00:06:14,332 --> 00:06:15,959
or the Miller Hook and platform?

116
00:06:17,919 --> 00:06:19,754
I think we're gonna
need a minute.

117
00:06:22,090 --> 00:06:24,301
Fun fact: it's neither.

118
00:06:25,927 --> 00:06:29,264
They actually use
the AAR type "E" coupler.

119
00:06:29,431 --> 00:06:31,766
If you listen carefully when
the locomotive disconnects,

120
00:06:31,933 --> 00:06:34,477
you'll hear the characteristic
click, whoosh,

121
00:06:34,644 --> 00:06:36,479
...thunk of the knuckle.

122
00:06:39,316 --> 00:06:41,109
Get out of town.

123
00:06:42,986 --> 00:06:45,739
Fun fact: I'm gonna
jump off this train.

124
00:06:50,243 --> 00:06:53,622
Hey, Cinnamon,
guess who just did it human style.

125
00:06:55,874 --> 00:06:57,500
Oh, my God!

126
00:06:57,667 --> 00:06:59,127
It's a little late, but I'll take it.

127
00:07:00,629 --> 00:07:03,757
No, Cinnamon ate the chocolates.
That's really bad for dogs.

128
00:07:03,924 --> 00:07:05,926
Oh, crap.
What are we gonna do?

129
00:07:06,092 --> 00:07:07,510
We gotta get her to a vet right now.

130
00:07:07,677 --> 00:07:09,846
I don't have a vet.
I have a podiatrist,

131
00:07:10,013 --> 00:07:13,016
an optometrist, an allergist,
a dermatologist, a urologist.

132
00:07:13,183 --> 00:07:14,851
You'd think I'd have a vet.

133
00:07:15,518 --> 00:07:16,728
OK. There's one not far from here.

134
00:07:16,895 --> 00:07:18,021
-Come on. Let's go.
-OK.

135
00:07:18,188 --> 00:07:20,982
Koothrappali was right.
We should have let her watch.

136
00:07:24,319 --> 00:07:27,072
Do another one, do another one!

137
00:07:27,238 --> 00:07:29,616
OK. Here's my impression
of the Amtrak Acela

138
00:07:29,783 --> 00:07:31,993
barreling down the Eastern Corridor.

139
00:07:32,202 --> 00:07:35,163
(MAKES CHUGGING
AND WHOOSHING SOUNDS)

140
00:07:40,168 --> 00:07:43,046
It's like there's a train
in your mouth.

141
00:07:44,756 --> 00:07:45,799
Oh, yeah. I've got one.

142
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:49,010
The Amtrak Wolverine
coming into Chicago.

143
00:07:50,428 --> 00:07:52,430
(MAKES PUFFING SOUNDS)

144
00:07:53,765 --> 00:07:56,017
(IMITATES WHISTLE BLOWING)

145
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:01,690
I've been on that train.
And I just was again.

146
00:08:04,025 --> 00:08:05,360
See if you guys can guess this one:

147
00:08:05,527 --> 00:08:07,946
Bang! Splat! Thud.

148
00:08:10,573 --> 00:08:12,283
How many trains
have you been on?

149
00:08:12,450 --> 00:08:16,037
Tons. A box fell on my
head at UPS six years ago.

150
00:08:16,204 --> 00:08:18,790
Now I just collect disability
and ride trains.

151
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:23,753
Wow. Your life's amazing.

152
00:08:24,754 --> 00:08:28,425
Not always. A box fell on my
head at UPS six years ago.

153
00:08:28,591 --> 00:08:31,678
Now I just collect disability
and ride trains.

154
00:08:36,766 --> 00:08:40,061
-Why do I even try?
-I'm gonna fix this right now.

155
00:08:40,228 --> 00:08:42,564
OK. Just make it
look like an accident.

156
00:08:45,942 --> 00:08:49,446
Excuse me. You are at Valentine's
dinner with your girlfriend.

157
00:08:49,612 --> 00:08:52,531
Now get back over there
and be with her.

158
00:08:53,616 --> 00:08:56,286
You're right.
That was insensitive of me.

159
00:08:57,412 --> 00:08:59,164
I have to go back to my table now.

160
00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:01,124
-You should join us.
-All right.

161
00:09:03,460 --> 00:09:06,713
Great. Now there's two of 'em.

162
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,677
-How much chocolate did she eat?
-A whole box.

163
00:09:12,844 --> 00:09:15,889
Well, to be fair, you ate a lot of it
before you gave it to me.

164
00:09:17,474 --> 00:09:19,142
So the point is I may
have saved her life.

165
00:09:21,436 --> 00:09:23,480
I'm sorry, is this a joke to you?

166
00:09:23,646 --> 00:09:25,106
No.

167
00:09:25,315 --> 00:09:26,775
Maybe to her.

168
00:09:29,444 --> 00:09:30,820
How big a box of chocolate was it?

169
00:09:30,987 --> 00:09:32,655
Something like this. I don't know.

170
00:09:32,822 --> 00:09:34,741
It came free with a full tank of gas.

171
00:09:36,201 --> 00:09:38,328
Really? Do you know how much
those Lakers tickets were?

172
00:09:38,495 --> 00:09:40,330
-Do you know how much gas is?
-Hey!

173
00:09:41,081 --> 00:09:42,499
-Sorry.
-Sorry.

174
00:09:44,292 --> 00:09:46,461
Oh, my God, Cinnamon,
are you OK?!

175
00:09:46,628 --> 00:09:48,296
I can't believe you two!

176
00:09:48,463 --> 00:09:49,923
You do whatever it takes
to save her life.

177
00:09:50,131 --> 00:09:52,050
If she needs new organs,
I'll buy any dog necessary

178
00:09:52,217 --> 00:09:54,010
and scrap them for parts!

179
00:09:55,553 --> 00:09:58,389
-You're the owner?
-Owner, father, soul mate,

180
00:09:58,556 --> 00:10:01,392
and if anything happens to her,
your worst nightmare!

181
00:10:04,062 --> 00:10:06,439
Well, she's not throwing up,
which is a good sign.

182
00:10:06,606 --> 00:10:08,983
So, I'm gonna take her in back,
put her on fluids

183
00:10:09,150 --> 00:10:10,735
and give her something
to absorb the toxins.

184
00:10:10,902 --> 00:10:12,403
OK. OK, thank you.

185
00:10:12,570 --> 00:10:15,907
If she's scared, you can sing to her.
She likes Katy Perry.

186
00:10:16,741 --> 00:10:19,327
But don't do "Firework"
That gets her all riled up.

187
00:10:20,203 --> 00:10:21,704
Got it.

188
00:10:22,288 --> 00:10:23,873
Should have been a dentist.

189
00:10:25,875 --> 00:10:29,796
OK, what was the best four-ten-four
US locomotive ever built?

190
00:10:29,963 --> 00:10:32,090
Trick question.
There never was one.

191
00:10:32,257 --> 00:10:35,468
-Or was there?
-What?!

192
00:10:37,387 --> 00:10:40,598
In 1944, the Pacific Railroad
built number 6131,

193
00:10:40,765 --> 00:10:42,517
which rearranged
the drivers and cylinders,

194
00:10:42,684 --> 00:10:44,477
solving the problem of the Q-1,

195
00:10:44,644 --> 00:10:46,437
creating a duplex-drive
four-four-six-four.

196
00:10:46,604 --> 00:10:50,316
In what world is a
four-four-six-four a four-ten-four?

197
00:10:50,483 --> 00:10:52,026
A world I don't want to live in!

198
00:10:54,154 --> 00:10:56,156
Seriously, I no longer want
to live in this world.

199
00:10:57,699 --> 00:11:00,785
Hold on to your conductor's hat.
You crank the second and third axles,

200
00:11:00,952 --> 00:11:03,121
creating a pair of
internal connecting rods,

201
00:11:03,288 --> 00:11:06,207
and boom! Four-ten-four.
If you think about it,

202
00:11:06,416 --> 00:11:08,793
the Q-2 was like the four-ten-four
America never made.

203
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:11,504
I may never stop thinking about it.

204
00:11:11,670 --> 00:11:14,007
Amy, what are the odds
we run into this guy?

205
00:11:14,174 --> 00:11:15,633
Better than you think.

206
00:11:19,012 --> 00:11:21,848
You know, if you ask nicely, they'll
let you visit the engine room.

207
00:11:23,057 --> 00:11:25,310
I never want this day to end.

208
00:11:25,476 --> 00:11:27,061
It's feeling like it never will.

209
00:11:28,687 --> 00:11:29,981
Come on.

210
00:11:30,773 --> 00:11:33,651
Hey, did I tell you what
happened to me at UPS?

211
00:11:36,404 --> 00:11:39,991
As if Valentine's Day wasn't bad
enough, you try to kill my dog?!

212
00:11:40,158 --> 00:11:42,160
And with cheap chocolate, no less?

213
00:11:42,327 --> 00:11:45,246
It wasn't cheap.
It was free.

214
00:11:46,539 --> 00:11:48,249
We're really sorry.
It was an accident.

215
00:11:48,416 --> 00:11:50,001
Yeah, we weren't even out
of the room that long.

216
00:11:50,168 --> 00:11:52,462
No, come on. It was a while.

217
00:11:54,339 --> 00:11:55,757
It was a while.

218
00:11:58,301 --> 00:12:00,970
-Is Cinnamon gonna be OK?
-She's responding well.

219
00:12:01,137 --> 00:12:03,598
We just want to keep her
a little longer for observation.

220
00:12:03,765 --> 00:12:06,059
All right. Would it be OK
for me to see her?

221
00:12:06,226 --> 00:12:09,103
We're usually in bed by now,
and I want her to know that I'm here.

222
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:12,899
Yes, we sleep together,
and sometimes we spoon.

223
00:12:15,734 --> 00:12:17,487
It's OK. I sleep with my dog, too.

224
00:12:18,655 --> 00:12:20,698
We're not supposed
to let people in back, but...

225
00:12:20,865 --> 00:12:22,325
...I think I can make an exception.

226
00:12:22,492 --> 00:12:24,327
-Thank you.
-Come on.

227
00:12:25,453 --> 00:12:28,164
-By the way, I sang her Katy Perry.
-Oh, yeah?

228
00:12:28,331 --> 00:12:32,168
And I don't care what that obnoxious
parrot back there says. I crushed it.

229
00:12:33,586 --> 00:12:35,754
I think there's something going
on between the two of them.

230
00:12:35,922 --> 00:12:38,424
Maybe, but you also think
nine minutes isn't a while,

231
00:12:38,591 --> 00:12:39,884
so what do you know?

232
00:12:47,475 --> 00:12:49,560
-You OK?
-Why?

233
00:12:49,727 --> 00:12:52,814
Because my boyfriend's off
playing choo-choo with some weirdo?

234
00:12:54,107 --> 00:12:57,318
Well, to be fair,
they're both weirdos.

235
00:12:58,611 --> 00:13:00,613
I don't know what made me think
tonight would be any different.

236
00:13:00,779 --> 00:13:04,158
Well, just the fact that you got
him up here still says a lot.

237
00:13:04,325 --> 00:13:06,619
To be honest, I bet Howie 200
bucks it wasn't gonna happen.

238
00:13:06,785 --> 00:13:09,788
I'm going to the LEGO store
to get a big-ass R2-D2.

239
00:13:13,876 --> 00:13:16,421
See? It's not just Sheldon.
They're all idiots.

240
00:13:18,631 --> 00:13:20,091
She's right.

241
00:13:21,175 --> 00:13:23,678
So your boyfriend's a fixer-upper.
Most of them are.

242
00:13:23,845 --> 00:13:26,180
I mean, look at this guy.
You think he came like this?

243
00:13:26,347 --> 00:13:28,516
When I met him,
he was a hot, goofy mess.

244
00:13:30,226 --> 00:13:32,353
Now, he's been to space.

245
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:34,272
That's all me.

246
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:39,485
-I had a little to do with it.
-Oh, sure you did.

247
00:13:39,652 --> 00:13:42,030
Who's Mama's big space man?

248
00:13:44,449 --> 00:13:46,117
I am.

249
00:13:52,790 --> 00:13:54,208
They've been in there,
like, half an hour.

250
00:13:54,375 --> 00:13:57,211
Yeah, for future reference,
that's a while.

251
00:14:01,966 --> 00:14:04,719
-How long should we stay?
-I don't know. I'm kind of hungry.

252
00:14:04,886 --> 00:14:08,264
-I saw a Thai place next door.
-OK.

253
00:14:10,308 --> 00:14:12,518
-How's she doing?
-How is everything?

254
00:14:12,685 --> 00:14:14,562
She's gonna be OK.
They're gonna let me take her home.

255
00:14:14,729 --> 00:14:15,772
Good.

256
00:14:15,938 --> 00:14:17,106
So I want you to keep
a close eye on her

257
00:14:17,273 --> 00:14:18,441
for the next 24 hours.

258
00:14:18,608 --> 00:14:20,985
Here are a few warning
signs to look out for.

259
00:14:21,152 --> 00:14:23,696
If you see any of them,
don't hesitate to call.

260
00:14:23,863 --> 00:14:26,699
Thank you for taking such
good care of my little girl.

261
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:28,785
My pleasure.
Have a nice night.

262
00:14:28,951 --> 00:14:30,620
Yes, thank you for all your help.

263
00:14:30,787 --> 00:14:32,747
Yeah. I'm sorry if you didn't think
we were taking it seriously.

264
00:14:32,914 --> 00:14:35,541
-We love animals.
-Do you guys own any pets?

265
00:14:35,708 --> 00:14:38,252
-No.
-Good.

266
00:14:45,301 --> 00:14:47,136
I love you so much.

267
00:14:47,303 --> 00:14:48,721
I love you, too.

268
00:14:57,021 --> 00:14:59,065
-Sorry.
-Sorry.

269
00:14:59,857 --> 00:15:01,651
You guys missed
a pretty great time.

270
00:15:01,818 --> 00:15:04,987
The conductor and I have
the same pocket watch.

271
00:15:06,781 --> 00:15:09,951
-It was crazy.
-Dare I say "loco?"

272
00:15:10,993 --> 00:15:12,412
Oh, and, Amy, guess what?

273
00:15:12,578 --> 00:15:14,330
The conductor said as
soon as he gets off work,

274
00:15:14,497 --> 00:15:17,792
he can come back to the bed and
breakfast and play his banjo for us.

275
00:15:19,335 --> 00:15:22,839
OK. I need to speak to my boyfriend
in private, like, right now.

276
00:15:23,005 --> 00:15:25,967
There's a car with a glass roof.
Want to go look at the stars?

277
00:15:26,134 --> 00:15:29,512
-That sounds so romantic.
-Give it a rest!

278
00:15:30,805 --> 00:15:32,265
Let's go.

279
00:15:36,394 --> 00:15:38,980
-Why are you still here?
-Excuse me.

280
00:15:39,147 --> 00:15:40,857
I think you're being a little rude.

281
00:15:41,023 --> 00:15:43,734
I'm being rude? You've been
rude to me this entire evening.

282
00:15:43,901 --> 00:15:45,445
How is that possible?

283
00:15:45,611 --> 00:15:48,739
I've hardly spoken
to you since we got on the train.

284
00:15:50,741 --> 00:15:52,910
I'm detecting a little
friction between you two,

285
00:15:53,077 --> 00:15:55,204
and I don't want to be a third rail.

286
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:57,415
Get it?

287
00:15:58,374 --> 00:15:59,625
I get it.

288
00:16:01,919 --> 00:16:03,296
Leave!

289
00:16:05,089 --> 00:16:07,800
-What is your problem?
-It's Valentine's Day.

290
00:16:07,967 --> 00:16:09,844
We're supposed to be
having a romantic weekend.

291
00:16:10,011 --> 00:16:12,513
Oh, really?
Because I remember you saying

292
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,849
that this trip was going to be
something we could both enjoy.

293
00:16:15,016 --> 00:16:18,144
Did you mean that,
or were you just trying to trick me?

294
00:16:18,936 --> 00:16:22,482
Fine, it's true.
I deserve romance,

295
00:16:22,648 --> 00:16:25,151
and I didn't know how
else to make it happen.

296
00:16:25,984 --> 00:16:30,198
Well, if you want romance,
then let's have romance!

297
00:16:30,364 --> 00:16:32,783
Oh, look, there's wine. Mm!

298
00:16:34,118 --> 00:16:36,537
Grape juice that burns.

299
00:16:36,704 --> 00:16:39,499
Now let's gaze
into each other's eyes.

300
00:16:39,665 --> 00:16:41,918
-You blinked. I win.
-Sheldon--

301
00:16:42,084 --> 00:16:44,545
Let's see. What's next?
Kissing's romantic.

302
00:17:02,855 --> 00:17:04,357
That was nice.

303
00:17:06,067 --> 00:17:07,610
Good.

304
00:17:09,612 --> 00:17:11,364
Um...

305
00:17:11,989 --> 00:17:15,409
The conductor said if I come
back to the engine room,

306
00:17:15,576 --> 00:17:19,038
he'd show me how to bring
the train through a crossing.

307
00:17:20,957 --> 00:17:23,084
OK, have fun.

308
00:17:25,253 --> 00:17:27,505
Do you want to come with me?

309
00:17:27,672 --> 00:17:30,091
Really? I do.

310
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:35,555
Hey, guys, wait up!

311
00:17:35,721 --> 00:17:37,515
(TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWS)

312
00:17:39,725 --> 00:17:43,646
There you go. All cozy wozy.

313
00:17:45,273 --> 00:17:49,610
Here, let's see what the doctor
says to keep an eye out for.

314
00:17:51,195 --> 00:17:53,781
Hm. "Rajesh, I was
dreading Valentine's Day.

315
00:17:53,948 --> 00:17:57,451
Thank you for spending it
with me. Yvette."

316
00:17:59,120 --> 00:18:03,207
Cinnamon, she gave me
her phone number.

317
00:18:03,374 --> 00:18:04,667
If I'd known it was that easy,
I would have considered

318
00:18:04,834 --> 00:18:06,627
poisoning you months ago.

319
00:18:08,963 --> 00:18:10,131
Oh, what should I say?

320
00:18:10,298 --> 00:18:11,757
Oh, I know.

321
00:18:11,924 --> 00:18:15,052
I'll point out her name's Yvette,
and that she's a vet.

322
00:18:15,219 --> 00:18:16,804
That's hysterical.

323
00:18:16,971 --> 00:18:18,347
She'll love it.

324
00:18:21,475 --> 00:18:23,060
-Hello.
-Hey. You're back.

325
00:18:23,227 --> 00:18:25,354
-How was your trip?
-It was wonderful!

326
00:18:25,521 --> 00:18:28,024
-Great. What did you do?
-I made a new friend

327
00:18:28,190 --> 00:18:30,026
who likes trains
as much as I do,

328
00:18:30,192 --> 00:18:31,777
I kissed Amy on the lips,

329
00:18:31,944 --> 00:18:34,780
and the conductor played his
banjo for me.

330
00:18:34,947 --> 00:18:36,365
Good night.

331
00:18:36,532 --> 00:18:39,702
Wait, wait, wait.
I'm gonna need more details.

332
00:18:39,869 --> 00:18:42,330
Well, my new
friend's name was Eric.

333
00:18:42,496 --> 00:18:46,083
Amy lips tasted like
the brownie we had for dessert.

334
00:18:47,335 --> 00:18:49,837
The banjo-playing
conductor was missing a finger,

335
00:18:50,004 --> 00:18:52,173
but he made up for it
with his can-do attitude.

336
00:18:53,257 --> 00:18:57,219
No, hang on. Hang on.
Are all those things equal to you?

337
00:18:57,386 --> 00:18:58,804
Hmm...

338
00:18:59,096 --> 00:19:01,265
It never occurred to me
to pick a favorite.

339
00:19:01,432 --> 00:19:03,559
Well, give it a go.

340
00:19:05,311 --> 00:19:08,397
I can't answer that without
collecting additional data.

341
00:19:09,482 --> 00:19:11,817
Additional data. You dog!

342
00:19:13,653 --> 00:19:16,238
I'm not sure how listening
to other nine-fingered banjo players

343
00:19:16,405 --> 00:19:18,407
makes me dog, but all right.

344
00:19:23,746 --> 00:19:27,124
(THEME MUSIC)

